---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getDataset } from '../lib/server/data';
import { formatRelative } from '../lib/core/dates';

const ds = getDataset();
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'How ExamLens verifies Tamil Nadu exam rules',
  description: 'The verification pipeline behind ExamLens: quoted citations, content hashes, dated diffs and explicit failure modes.',
  dateModified: ds.generatedAt ?? undefined,
};
---
<Base
  title="Method — how each rule is verified | ExamLens"
  description="ExamLens quotes every rule from the official notification, re-downloads the source on a schedule, and fails visibly when a document changes."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="wrap narrow" style="padding-block:clamp(1.6rem,4vw,2.6rem)">
    <p class="section-label">Method</p>
    <h1>How we know a rule is still true</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      An age limit is easy to copy. Establishing whether it is still correct requires a record of
      where it came from and a check that the source has not changed. This page describes that
      process, including its limits.
    </p>

    <section class="method-section">
      <h2>1. Every fact carries a quote</h2>
      <p>
        An age limit, a qualification clause or a fee is stored with a <strong>verbatim sentence</strong> from the
        notification and the page it appears on. Where a value sits in a table cell rather than a
        sentence, the table's own heading is stored together with the numbers, and the check confirms
        that those numbers are still printed on that page.
      </p>
      <p>
        On every run the pipeline downloads the document again (conditionally, when the server supports
        it), extracts its text layer and searches for the quotation. If the search fails, the
        examination is flagged: a warning appears on the page, the eligibility answer is withheld, and
        the build records the failure. Nothing is silently rewritten.
      </p>
      <div class="pipeline">
        <div><b>fetch</b><span>HTML + PDF, conditional GET</span></div>
        <div><b>extract</b><span>text layer, page by page</span></div>
        <div><b>verify</b><span>quote + numeric fingerprint</span></div>
        <div><b>diff</b><span>against the stored snapshot</span></div>
        <div><b>publish</b><span>static JSON + pages</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="method-section">
      <h2>2. The change log is a diff, not a rewrite</h2>
      <p>
        The pipeline keeps the previous state of every exam: its document list (category, title, URL, upload date)
        and the raw date lines from the board dashboard. A new addendum, a changed exam-date line, a withdrawn
        document or a revised vacancy count is a difference. Differences become dated entries.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where the document states the change explicitly — “the preliminary examination … has been postponed from
        06.09.2026 to 27.09.2026” — the sentence is stored with the entry as evidence, alongside the PDF it came from.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="method-section">
      <h2>3. Age is computed, not compared to a number</h2>
      <p>
        Every notification has an <em>as on</em> date, and the rule is “should not have completed N years”.
        ExamLens computes completed years on that exact date, applies the community ceiling, then applies the
        most favourable concession the candidate is entitled to (PwBD, ex-servicemen, destitute widow).
        Minimum-age failures show the date you turn eligible; maximum-age failures show how far past the cut-off you are.
      </p>
      <p>
        The engine is a pure function with unit tests on real notification values, so the same input always
        produces the same answer — and the answer cites the paragraph it used.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="method-section">
      <h2>4. What this cannot do</h2>
      <ul class="limits">
        <li><strong>It cannot read Tamil-language PDFs reliably.</strong> Some board PDFs have text layers that extract as garbage. Those are monitored by hash, not by quote, and labelled as such.</li>
        <li><strong>It cannot know about a post-specific rule hidden in an annexure</strong> unless that rule has been transcribed and cited. Records without verified rules say “dates tracked only” and give no verdict.</li>
        <li><strong>It cannot replace the notification.</strong> Fees, document formats, physical standards and certificate validity are not modelled.</li>
        <li><strong>It can lag.</strong> If a board publishes between runs, this record is behind until the next scheduled check. Every page states when its sources were last read.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="method-section" id="corrections">
      <h2>5. Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        The notification is the authority. Where this record disagrees with it, this record is wrong.
        Report the notification number and the paragraph through the repository's issue tracker; the
        entry is corrected and re-verified on the next run. Corrections pass through the same quotation
        check as every other entry.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Data licence: CC BY 4.0 for the compilation. Official notices and PDFs remain the copyright of their issuing authority.
        ExamLens is independent and not affiliated with TNPSC, TRB, TNUSRB or the Government of Tamil Nadu.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="tiny muted">Last rebuilt {ds.generatedAt ? formatRelative(ds.generatedAt) : '—'} · {ds.counts.verifiedExams} verified records · {ds.counts.sources} board endpoints.</p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .method-section { margin-bottom: 34px; }
  .method-section h2 { font-size: 1.3rem; }
  .method-section p, .method-section li { color: var(--ink-2); }
  .pipeline { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr); gap: 8px; margin: 18px 0 4px; }
  @media (max-width: 720px) { .pipeline { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
  .pipeline div { border: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 8px 10px; background: #fff; }
  .pipeline b { display: block; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--link); }
  .pipeline span { font-size: 0.76rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .limits { padding-left: 18px; display: grid; gap: 8px; }
</style>
